  "reason": "The records collectively establish several relevant elements: temperature-dependent MD deformation of closely related FCC CoCrFeNiMn alloys, lower dislocation density and strength at about 1000 K, increased HCP fractions at high temperature, links among partial dislocations, stacking faults and FCC-HCP transformation, and elevated-temperature reductions in stiffness. Separate records also demonstrate nanoindentation of HEAs. Thus they point toward studying temperature-dependent indentation and interpreting the response through dislocation and lattice/phase evolution. However, none discloses the specific FeNiCrCoCu coating-on-Cu geometry, combined nanoindentation/friction simulation across 300–1200 K, the sharp disorder-driven transition above roughly 900 K, or the resulting rise in friction. Combining those elements would require a substantial additional idea, so the result is only partially reconstructible."
